result_text:
I found 8 issues in the diff. Two concern the correctness of the new encoder, and the remaining six are cleanup items.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "src/DataProtection/DataProtection/src/KeyManagement/KeyRingBasedDataProtector.cs",
    "line": 397,
    "summary": "The rented ArrayPool<int> buffer is not returned inside try/finally, so any exception between Rent and Return leaks it.",
    "failure_scenario": "A purpose contains a lone surrogate (e.g. \"\\uD800\"). SecureUtf8Encoding.GetByteCount throws EncoderFallbackException at line 370, so Return at line 397 never runs. A caller that keeps retrying CreateProtector with bad input drains the pool, and each failed call falls back to a fresh allocation. The old MemoryStream path had no pooled resource to leak."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/DataProtection/DataProtection/src/KeyManagement/KeyRingBasedDataProtector.cs",
    "line": 374,
    "summary": "The total AAD length is summed with unchecked int arithmetic. On overflow it wraps instead of throwing, which the old MemoryStream path would have done.",
    "failure_scenario": "Purposes whose combined UTF-8 byte count exceeds int.MaxValue make totalPurposeLen wrap to a negative or small value. The result is OverflowException at `new byte[]` or an ArgumentOutOfRange/IndexOutOfRange from Slice/GetBytes, not a clear error. The old code went through checked((int)...) and MemoryStream's capacity checks."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/DataProtection/DataProtection/src/KeyManagement/KeyRingBasedDataProtector.cs",
    "line": 356,
    "summary": "The new path is gated on `#if NET10_0_OR_GREATER`, but every API it uses exists on any NETCOREAPP target, and this file already uses `#if NETCOREAPP` at lines 144 and 297.",
    "failure_scenario": "It is inconsistent with the rest of the file. If the default TFM is ever multi-targeted with an older netcoreapp, that build silently falls back to the slow MemoryStream path. Use `#if NETCOREAPP` to match the existing guards."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/DataProtection/DataProtection/src/KeyManagement/KeyRingBasedDataProtector.cs",
    "line": 356,
    "summary": "The AAD serialization now exists twice (span-based for net10, BinaryWriter for netfx/netstandard2.0), and no test checks that the two produce identical bytes.",
    "failure_scenario": "The AAD is part of the authenticated-encryption contract. If a later edit to one branch makes the bytes differ, payloads protected on net10 will not unprotect on net462/netstandard2.0, and vice versa. A simpler option is one span-based implementation for all TFMs, since BinaryPrimitives and ArrayPool are available via System.Memory/System.Buffers on netstandard2.0. The only TFM-specific piece would be LeadingZeroCount, which a loop can replace."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/DataProtection/DataProtection/test/Microsoft.AspNetCore.DataProtection.Tests/KeyManagement/KeyRingBasedDataProtectorTests.cs",
    "line": 35,
    "summary": "No test exercises the new hand-written 7-bit length encoder with multi-byte lengths or non-ASCII purposes. Existing tests only use short ASCII purposes.",
    "failure_scenario": "An off-by-one in Measure7BitEncodedUIntLength or Write7BitEncodedInt at the 127/128 or 16383/16384 byte boundaries, or with multi-byte UTF-8 characters, would change the AAD bytes. That silently breaks unprotection of existing payloads, and the suite would not catch it. Add tests with a 128+ byte purpose and a non-ASCII purpose, comparing against BinaryWriter output."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/DataProtection/DataProtection/src/KeyManagement/KeyRingBasedDataProtector.cs",
    "line": 365,
    "summary": "Renting an int[] from ArrayPool just to cache per-purpose byte counts adds complexity, and it is the only reason a try/finally is needed.",
    "failure_scenario": "Purpose arrays are tiny (usually 1-5 entries). A simpler, allocation-free form is `Span<int> lengths = purposes.Length <= 16 ? stackalloc int[16] : new int[purposes.Length];`. Calling GetByteCount again in the second loop would also work. Either way the Rent/Return bookkeeping and the leak risk go away."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/DataProtection/DataProtection/src/KeyManagement/KeyRingBasedDataProtector.cs",
    "line": 19,
    "summary": "`using System.Buffers.Text;` is never used. The new usings are also out of order: System.Buffers.Binary comes before System.Buffers, and System.Buffers.Text sits after the Microsoft.* usings.",
    "failure_scenario": "On netstandard2.0/net462, where none of these usings are needed, the unused directive raises IDE0005/CS8019 warnings, which may fail builds that treat warnings as errors. It also adds noise and violates the repo's sorted-using convention."
  },
  {
    "file": "src/DataProtection/DataProtection/src/KeyManagement/KeyRingBasedDataProtector.cs",
    "line": 407,
    "summary": "A commented-out loop is left as dead code after a return statement in Measure7BitEncodedUIntLength. The magic numbers 4 and `4 + keySize + 4` are used instead of sizeof(uint), and the variable totalPurposeLen actually holds the total AAD length.",
    "failure_scenario": "This makes maintenance harder. The dead code below the return is misleading, and the hard-coded offsets duplicate layout knowledge that GetAadForKey expresses as sizeof(uint)/sizeof(Guid). If the header layout changes, those offsets can drift out of sync."
  }
]
```
